  [1m8. Testing[0m

  [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, no dependencies, via [38;5;153mnpm test[39m. Covers: redaction of flat/nested/array/cyclic fields, level filtering,
  record shape, both username policies (determinism and distinctness), and that a throwing sink doesn't propagate. Both
  entry modules export factories taking injected [38;5;153msink[39m/[38;5;153mstorage[39m, so the browser policy is tested under Node without a
  browser.

  [1m9. One consequence of ESM you should know about[0m

  Converting [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m means the page [1mstops working when opened as a [38;5;153mfile://[39m URL[0m — module
  scripts are CORS-restricted. You'll need to serve it ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m, say). I'll note it in the README. This
  is inherent to the ESM choice, not to the logger.

  [1m10. Out of scope[0m

  Remote shipping, batching/retry, sampling, cross-request correlation IDs, consent UI. The sink seam is where these
  land later; none are built now.

  [1mApprove this and I'll write it up as a spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-logging-subsystem-design.md[0m
  (uncommitted), run it through the spec review gate, and hand it to you before any code gets written. The two things
  I'd most like a reaction to are §4 (the hash is not a privacy guarantee) and §7's rule that program output stays off
  the logger.

